course: euphemisms
lesson: 27
title: Soft words for hard things: crime, prison and the police
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Prison, crime and police violence are hard things, so people find softer words for them.
explain: Officials use long, tidy words to hide what is happening: a prison becomes a "correctional facility", a police shooting becomes an "officer-involved shooting".
explain: Ordinary people use slang and jokes instead: someone in prison is "inside" or "doing time", and a thief has "sticky fingers".
explain: A vivid example: shops call stolen goods "shrinkage", as if the stock just got smaller by itself.
explain: When you hear such words, ask: who is in prison, who stole, who used force, and who is being protected by the soft word?
fact: "Correctional facility" is the US official word for a prison.
fact: "Correctional officer" is the US official word for a prison guard.
fact: "Detained at His Majesty's pleasure" is a British official phrase for being held in prison for an indefinite time, with no end date fixed in advance.
fact: "Guest of His Majesty" is a British joke for a prisoner.
fact: "Doing time" means serving a prison sentence.
fact: "Inside" is British everyday talk for in prison.
fact: "Away" can mean in prison, as in "Her dad's away for a while".
fact: "Up the river" is an old US phrase for in prison.
fact: "Helping police with their inquiries" is a British phrase for being questioned as a suspect (a person the police think may have done the crime).
fact: "Person of interest" is the US official phrase for a suspect.
fact: "Known to police" means a person has a criminal record (a police list of crimes they were found guilty of).
fact: "Administrative segregation" is the US official phrase for solitary confinement (keeping a prisoner alone in a cell, away from everyone, as a punishment).
fact: "Protective custody" means keeping a prisoner alone for their own safety.
fact: "Use of force" is the official phrase for violence by police.
fact: "Officer-involved shooting" is a US official phrase meaning police shot someone.
fact: "Less-lethal weapon" means a weapon meant not to kill, such as a rubber bullet, which can still kill.
fact: "Justice-involved" is a US official phrase for having a criminal record or past contact with the courts.
fact: "Sticky fingers" means a habit of stealing.
fact: "Five-finger discount" is a US joke for shoplifting.
fact: "Shrinkage" is shop jargon for stock lost to theft by staff and customers.
exercise: What is a "correctional facility"? | A prison | A school for spelling | A repair shop for glasses | A gym
exercise: What does a "correctional officer" do? | Guards prisoners in a prison | Marks spelling tests | Fixes broken machines | Corrects mistakes in newspapers
exercise: What does "detained at His Majesty's pleasure" mean in Britain? | Held in prison for an indefinite time | Invited to tea at the palace | Kept late after school by the King | Working as the King's servant
exercise: If a Briton jokes that someone is a "guest of His Majesty", where is that person? | In prison | Staying at the royal palace | At a royal garden party | At court
exercise: What does "doing time" mean? | Serving a prison sentence | Learning to tell the time | Working long hours at a job | Waiting for a bus
exercise: In British talk, what does it mean that someone is "inside"? | They are in prison | They are in their house | They are in hospital | They are sitting indoors because of rain
exercise: "Her dad's away for a couple of years." In crime talk, what does "away" mean? | In prison | On a long holiday | Working on a ship | Abroad
exercise: In old US talk, what does "up the river" mean? | In prison | Fishing on a boat | Living in the mountains | Lost
exercise: A British news report says a man is "helping police with their inquiries". What is happening? | He is being questioned as a suspect | He is working as a police helper | He is giving police directions | He is a police officer on duty
exercise: What is a "person of interest" to US police? | A suspect | A famous person | A very interesting guest | A witness
exercise: What does "known to police" mean? | The person has a criminal record | The person is friends with the police | The person is a police officer | The person once called the police
exercise: What is "administrative segregation" in a US prison? | Solitary confinement | Office work for prisoners | A meeting of prison managers | Sorting letters for prisoners
exercise: What is "protective custody" in prison? | Keeping a prisoner alone for their own safety | Locking up the prison guards | Putting a prisoner in a soft room to play | Letting a prisoner go home early
exercise: What does "use of force" usually mean in police reports? | Violence by police | A science lesson on forces | Police using a strong car | Pushing a stuck door open
exercise: What is an "officer-involved shooting"? | Police shot someone | Police took photographs | A shooting contest for officers | An officer was shot by accident at practice
exercise: What is a "less-lethal weapon"? | A weapon meant not to kill, which still can | A toy weapon that cannot hurt | A broken weapon | A weapon with no bullets in it
exercise: What does "justice-involved" mean in US official talk? | Having a criminal record or court past | Working as a judge | Loving fairness | Being on a jury
exercise: If someone has "sticky fingers", what are they like? | They steal things | They eat too much honey | They are good at climbing | They forget to wash their hands
exercise: What is a "five-finger discount"? | Shoplifting | A sale of gloves | A price cut of five per cent | A manicure
exercise: What does a shop manager mean by "shrinkage"? | Stock lost to theft | Clothes that shrink in the wash | Prices getting smaller | A smaller shop building
test: News: "A 34-year-old man is helping police with their inquiries after the robbery." What is really happening? | He is being questioned as a suspect in the robbery | He is a volunteer detective | He found the stolen money | He is a witness who saw nothing
test: "Shut up about my brother. He's inside, all right? Three years." Where is the brother? | In prison for three years | Sitting indoors for three years | In hospital | At a boarding school
test: Shop owner: "Shrinkage cost us two thousand pounds this year." What happened? | Goods worth that much were stolen | The shop building got smaller | Prices dropped by that much | Clothes shrank in the wash
test: Gossip: "Watch your purse round Dave, he's got sticky fingers." What is the warning? | Dave steals | Dave has been eating jam | Dave is clumsy | Dave is good at catching things
test: A US news line: "Police have named a person of interest in the case." Which is true? | Police suspect that person | Police have found a witness to thank | Police have found a famous guest | Police have arrested the victim
test: "The suspect is known to police," says the reporter. What does it tell you? | He has a criminal record | He is a police officer | He is a friend of the police chief | He is a lost child
test: Report: "Officers used a less-lethal weapon to stop the man." Which is true? | They used a weapon meant not to kill, which can still hurt or kill | They used a toy | They used no weapon at all | They used a deadly gun on purpose
test: Mate: "lol he got a five-finger discount on them sweets." What did he do? | He stole the sweets | He got a coupon | He paid half price | He bought five sweets
test: "Her uncle's been a guest of His Majesty since last spring," laughs a neighbour. Where is the uncle? | In prison | At the King's palace | At sea | In the army
test: A US prison report: "The inmate was placed in administrative segregation." What happened to him? | He was put in solitary confinement | He was given an office job | He was set free | He was moved to a bigger cell with friends
test: "My cousin did time, back in the 90s — no wait, early 2000s." What did the cousin do? | Served a prison sentence in the early 2000s | Worked as a clockmaker in the 90s | Served a prison sentence in the 90s | Studied history in the early 2000s
test: A police statement says "an officer-involved shooting occurred on Main Street". What happened? | A police officer shot someone | An officer was hit by a car | An officer took photos | There was a shooting game at a fair
test: Old film: "Ten years up the river, that's what you'll get!" What is the threat? | Ten years in prison | Ten years of fishing | Ten years living on a boat | Ten years of rowing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phrase "detained at His Majesty's pleasure"? | Held in prison for a period not fixed in advance | Received as a guest at a royal residence | Retained in the employment of the Crown | Kept late at school by royal command
exam: Identify the principal meaning of "use of force" as employed in police reports. | Violence by police | The application of physics in the classroom | The operation of police vehicles | The opening of jammed doors
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of "protective custody" within a prison. | The isolation of a prisoner for his own safety | The confinement of the prison staff | The early release of a prisoner | The placing of a prisoner in a recreation room
exam: It is given that an applicant is described as "justice-involved". Ascertain what is thereby indicated. | The applicant has a criminal record or past dealings with the courts | The applicant serves as a judge | The applicant has sat upon a jury | The applicant holds strong views upon fairness
